cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use the proof, its mathematical compression route, the tooling or the measurements, please cite this paper-repository (see also paper/references.bib)."
title: "Library-Native Proof Compression: Successive Record Formalizations of the Classical Zero-Free Region in Lean 4"
abstract: "A research paper (English and Chinese) on library-native proof compression for the classical de la Vallee Poussin zero-free region of the Riemann zeta function in Lean 4 / Mathlib. Five successive record-breaking formalizations are analyzed: the artifact shrank 3,274 -> 3,208 -> 3,065 -> 3,014 -> 2,931 normalized tokens (official scores 64.5332 -> 64.6400 -> 64.8928 -> 64.9800 -> 65.1339, all with curve flag kept=true), against a 21,641-token / 21-file reference (86.5% reduction). Each record step is traced to a structural insight: prescribed witness gaps; a recursive zero-peeling lemma built from analytic divided differences (dslope) and Borel-Caratheodory replacing finite zero products and contour estimates; a Mellin entire formula yielding both growth and a cheap zero exclusion; an adaptive scale a = Re s + 5d with d = 2^-20/log R making every estimate crude yet decisive; and successive fusions of the window/assembly interface. The paper includes component-level token accounting, an ablation showing the Euler-Maclaurin growth route costs ~50% more formal tokens, official evaluation, and the platform technique note core1_borel_dslope reproduced in both languages."
